    },
    {
      "text": "in an IDE, the order is wrong and so you're starting with the code, but now it's time to transition to like working with teammates where you chat first and you dig in when you need it",
      "timestamp_sec": 516,
      "why": "Specific, arguable critique of the IDE-first agent paradigm."
    },
    {
      "text": "Codex cannot be a closed product that only OpenAI can improve. So we've intentionally designed Codex as a set of layers that anyone can build on.",
      "timestamp_sec": 579,
      "why": "The openness strategy stated as a design constraint, not a marketing line."
    },
    {
      "text": "Codex needed a way to compact long contexts for long-running tasks and so we'll build that into the API. So that means your agents can use the same primitives that we built for ourselves.",
      "timestamp_sec": 615,
      "why": "Concrete example of the internal-first-then-API pattern."
    },
    {
      "text": "We're not building one system for Open AI and a second system that's simplified for developers. At every layer, we actually use the thing that we give to you.",
      "timestamp_sec": 792,
      "why": "The single takeaway the speaker explicitly flags as the section's point."
    },
    {
      "text": "at only $1 per million input tokens and $6 per million output tokens. I'll leave it up to you to compare those costs, but that is insane value.",
      "timestamp_sec": 929,
      "why": "Hard pricing numbers for the cost-efficiency claim."
    },
    {
      "text": "this is GPT 5.6 Soul running on Cerebras, the frontier intelligence at now 750 tokens a second",
      "timestamp_sec": 929,
      "why": "The headline throughput figure of the talk."
    },
    {
      "text": "the future shouldn't have this awkward distinction between like a local task and a cloud task, and you have to decide where to run everything",
      "timestamp_sec": 1037,
      "why": "A forward-looking position on agent execution environments."
    },
    {
      "text": "I thought I was orchestrating. Really, I was polling. I was the scheduler, the router, and the memory.",
      "timestamp_sec": 1159,
      "why": "The sharpest reframing of multi-terminal agent workflows in the talk."
    },
    {
      "text": "I manage the manager of a small company of agents.",
      "timestamp_sec": 1159,
      "why": "One-line statement of the delegation architecture being advocated."
    },
    {
      "text": "So, we have persistent context, delegation, and triggers. There's your loop.",
      "timestamp_sec": 1214,
      "why": "Names the three ingredients as a reusable recipe."
    },
    {
      "text": "last year, I was primarily constrained by tokens. Now, I fixed that by joining OpenAI. I know I know the strategy does not scale.",
      "timestamp_sec": 1214,
      "why": "Self-aware framing of the moving-bottleneck argument."
    },
    {
      "text": "Now, I'm primarily constrained by attention. And unlike tokens or compute, I can't simply add more of it.",
      "timestamp_sec": 1276,
      "why": "The conclusion of the bottleneck progression and the talk's practical thesis."
    },
    {
      "text": "the latest generation of models is so good at understanding intent that it's a little bit of a waste of time to watch the agent generate code",
      "timestamp_sec": 1276,
      "why": "Direct, checkable advice that contradicts common supervise-the-agent practice."
    },
    {
      "text": "The agent runs the inner execution loop. I set the direction and I make decisions in the outer loop.",
      "timestamp_sec": 1323,
      "why": "Cleanest statement of the human-agent division of labor."
    },
    {
      "text": "The manager shouldn't be a session trapped inside your app. It should be an agent that I can text, steer from Slack, or hear from wherever I am.",
      "timestamp_sec": 1437,
      "why": "Specifies what current harnesses are missing."
    },
    {
      "text": "Models are advancing faster than the harnesses and organizations around them. Designing those things is the next engineering problem.",
      "timestamp_sec": 1437,
      "why": "The call to action and the talk's framing of where value now accrues."
    }
